  "title": "Capacity",
  "emoji": "🥤",
  "parentNote": "Use a measuring jug and a few bottles/cups at bath time or in the kitchen — capacity is easiest to learn by actually pouring water.",
  "learnBullets": [
    "Units of capacity: **millilitre (mL)** and **litre (L)**",
    "The conversion rule: **1 L = 1000 mL**",
    "Adding and subtracting capacities",
    "Comparing and ordering capacities"
  ],
  "learnItFirst": "```\n1 litre (L)  =  1000 millilitres (mL)\n```\n\n**Converting litres to millilitres:** multiply by 1000\n```\n2 L = 2 × 1000 = 2000 mL\n```\n\n**Converting millilitres to litres:** divide by 1000 (works evenly for multiples of 1000)\n```\n4000 mL = 4000 ÷ 1000 = 4 L\n```\n\n**Adding/subtracting capacities** — keep the unit the same:\n```\n250 mL + 300 mL = 550 mL\n1 L 200 mL  =  1200 mL   (1 L = 1000 mL, so 1000 + 200 = 1200)\n```\n\n### 🧠 How to Solve — 3 Easy Steps\n1. **Check the units** — are both amounts in mL, or in L? Make them match first.\n2. **Convert** using 1 L = 1000 mL if needed.\n3. **Add, subtract, or compare** the numbers normally.\n\n---",
